---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const SV = "server/src/iki_server/signatures/services.py";
const P = "server/src/iki_server/signatures/proof.py";
const EN = "server/src/iki_server/signatures/entries.py";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "A signature sets a state, bound to one revision" },
  { id: "how", label: "Signing, step by step" },
  { id: "reversals", label: "Leaving a signed state" },
  { id: "rules", label: "The rules in full" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Routes, files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="A signature sets a state, bound to one revision">
    <p>
      A state can need electronic signatures (21 CFR Part 11). It's never set directly: each signer
      signs in again at the identity provider, Iki signs a canonical document naming the SHA-256 of
      the record's content at that revision with the deployment's Ed25519 key, and the last signature
      sets the state, on a revision that carries them all. A signature counts only while the record
      stays at the revision it signed. Once a state has been entered by signing, a return to an
      earlier signed state needs a reason, and leaving it for an unsigned one needs a signed reversal
      by someone holding a lab role the original signature recorded, with a reason (<Adr n={[124, 126]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="Signing, step by step">
    <p>
      A state's <code>signatures</code> are slots, each with a meaning (authorship, review,
      approval…), a statement the signer reads, and optionally a label and its own lab role;
      without a role, a slot is given by anyone the state's <code>set_by</code> allows. Whether one
      person may give two, or in what order, is the plugin's, as guards.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Before anyone signs.</strong> An Org Admin records the organization's certification
        letter to the FDA (§11.100(c)); with none, nobody signs. Each person agrees, at their first
        signature, that it binds them as their handwritten one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Begin.</strong> The signer starts a short-lived <code>Signing</code> (ten minutes) in
        the web app for the revision they're reading, after its role, guard and <code
          >recheck_before_signing</code
        > rule checks pass (<Path p={SV} sym="def begin(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign in again.</strong> Iki sends them to the identity provider with a forced fresh
        sign-in: OIDC <code>prompt=login</code> and <code>max_age=0</code>; SAML <code
          >ForceAuthn</code
        >. The answer must be fresh and for the same person (<Path
          p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/reauth.py"
        />). Three failures in 15 minutes lock their signing until an Org Admin unlocks it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign.</strong> On return, under the row lock, the revision must not have moved. Iki
        builds the document: the record (ID, type, HRID, revision, its entry's revision, and its
        content's SHA-256), the change (field, from, to, and whether it's a reversal), the slot's
        terms, the signer, the reason, when, how they signed in again, and the public key. It's
        canonical JSON (RFC 8785), signed with Ed25519 (<Path p={SV} sym="def _sign(" />, <Path
          p={P}
          sym="def canonical("
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The last one sets the state.</strong> When every slot counts, the state is set by{" "}
        <code>states.write</code>, and its revision carries the signatures in order. Each step is
        audited: <code>signature.requested</code>, <code>signature.signed</code>, <code
          >signature.failed</code
        >.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The content is the record's values as its history keeps them, with each quantity's unit,
      canonical JSON too. A record of a registry entry is signed with its entry's revision, so a
      change to the entry lapses it as well.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reversals">Leaving a signed state</h3>
    <p>
      Iki reads, from the record's own state history, which signatures entered the state each field
      is in now (<Path p={EN} sym="def for_records(" />). While that state was entered by signing,
      the field's other states change (<Path p={EN} sym="class Entry:" />):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A return to a signed state it's been in before</strong> uses that state's
        signatures, and the signer also says why.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A move to an unsigned state</strong> becomes a reversal: one slot per signature that
        entered the current state, each keeping its meaning, labelled "Reversal of …", with the
        statement "I reverse the decision signed as …", and a reason required. Each signer must hold
        one of the lab roles the original signature recorded and be allowed to set the destination
        (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/changes.py" sym="def roles_for(" />). The signed
        document marks it <code>reversal</code>, with both states and the revision. A slot whose
        original signature can't be read can't be given.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Then every value edit</strong> after the record is unlocked carries its editor's
        reason: <PageLink to="Locks and corrections" />.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A forward step to a state the record hasn't been in uses its declared signatures and reason
      policy. The <PageLink to="States and guards" hash="example" /> worked example, a plasmid
      verification's review, shows a released record reversed to Recorded by QA.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="The rules in full">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="When a signature counts" open>
        <p>
          A signature counts while the record, and its entry, stay at the revisions it signed, while
          its signer still holds a role it needs, and while the plugin's terms for its slot are what
          they were. Once the record changes it has lapsed, and the change needs it again. A signer
          can withdraw theirs, with a reason, before the state is set (<Path
            p={SV}
            sym="def withdraw("
          />). Guards run again at each signature, with the signer acting and the signatures given so
          far (<Adr n="0128" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Verifying a signature">
        <p>
          <code>GET /api/v1/signatures/{"{id}"}/verification</code> answers with each check: the
          document is the format it says; its Ed25519 signature matches; the content it names hashes
          to what it signed; the record's history, recomputed now, is still that content; and the
          revision that set the state names it. A value changed in SQL, a signature moved to another
          record, a document edited or a signature deleted each fail a named check (<Path
            p={SV}
            sym="def verify("
          />, <Path p={P} />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Verification reads through renames and unit conversions that migrations recorded in <code
            >iki_history_change</code
          >, each vouched for by the deployment's key, instead of calling them tampering (<Adr
            n="0148"
          />, <Path p={P} sym="def read_through(" />). A bundle carries its signatures and verifies
          offline with <code>iki bundle verify</code>: <PageLink to="Exports" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What's kept, and kept for good">
        <p>
          Signatures, withdrawals, certifications and acknowledgments are append-only tables (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/models.py"
            sym="class Signature("
          />). The deployment's key is made at the first signature, its private half kept encrypted
          with <code>IKI_ENCRYPTION_KEYS</code>, so a key is trusted only once its private half
          decrypts and gives its public one. <code>iki-server signing_key</code> retires it for a new
          one, audited as <code>signature.key_rotated</code> (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/signing_keys.py"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A release signs the review it releases">
        <p>
          When a result's release needs signing and its review was signed, QA's document names the
          reviewer's revision, its content digest and the signatures that established review (<Adr
            n="0179"
          />). <PageLink to="Release and completion" /> covers what the requesting project receives.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Routes, files and decisions">
    <Routes tags={["signatures"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={SV} />: begin, finish, verify, withdraw</li>
          <li><Path p={P} />: the document and its checks, without Django</li>
          <li><Path p={EN} />: signed entries and their reversals</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/reauth.py" />: signing in again</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0124" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0126" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0148" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
